      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To compare serum lipids and their changes during a family-based health education in children aged 6-17 years with or without the epsilon4 allele of the gene encoding apolipoprotein E (apoE)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DESIGN",
          "content": "An intervention study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SETTING",
          "content": "A family-based prevention of risk factors of coronary heart disease in Eastern Finland. The programme consisted of two counselling meetings at children's schools and three at children's homes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "SUBJECTS",
          "content": "Four hundred and thirty-nine children with a family history of cardiovascular diseases (CVD) participated in a family-based health education. The children were divided into two groups according to apoE genotype. The risk group consisted of 143 children having apoE epsilon4 allele (genotype epsilon3/4 or epsilon4/4) and the non-risk group of 296 children without apoE epsilon4 allele (epsilon2/3 or epsilon3/3). The final sample of the follow-up study included 354 (81%) children (114 and 240, respectively)."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Baseline differences were found in low-density lipoprotein cholesterol (LDL-C) (P=0.007) and LDL-C/high-density lipoprotein cholesterol (HDL-C) ratio (P=0.030) among boys and in total cholesterol (TC)/HDL-C (P=0.008) and LDL-C/HDL-C ratios (P=0.006) among girls. Differences between groups in changes during the follow-up were observed only for TC/HDL-C ratio (P-value adjusted for age=0.049) among boys."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "At baseline, children with apoE epsilon4 allele had on average a more unfavourable lipid profile than those without apoE epsilon4 allele. However, the effect of about 33 months' family-based health education on plasma lipids did not depend on apoE genotype in children with a family history of CVD."
        }
      ]},
